    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay that makes sense, but i'm still stuck on the \"how\" part. if i have a messy, complicated signal—like a recording of a noisy room—how does the math actually \"pick out\" a single frequency from all that noise? what is the mechanism that separates one wave from another when they are all tangled up together?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Fourier Transform uses a mathematical technique called convolution to separate the signals. It essentially computes the \"cross-power spectral density\" between the signal and a shifting sine wave, essentially asking: \"where in the time series would a sine wave have maximum correlation with our original signal?\" This process allows the transform to pick out individual frequencies by finding the peaks in the frequency spectrum.",
